{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Give ear, O heavens, etc.</b> There are several points to note. First: why did he say ״וַאֲדַבֵּרָה״, and not say in this manner ״הַאֲזִינוּ הַשָּׁמַיִם דְּבָרַי״? Second: why did he not include them together and say ״הַאֲזִינוּ הַשָּׁמַיִם וְהָאָרֶץ״, or ״שִׁמְעוּ שָׁמַיִם וָאָרֶץ״? Third: why for the heavens did he use the language of הַאֲזָנָה and for the earth the language of שְׁמִיעָה? Fourth: why for the heavens did he use the language of command — ״הַאֲזִינוּ״ in the imperative, while for the earth he said ״וְתִשְׁמַע״ when he should have said ״וְשִׁמְעִי הָאָרֶץ״? Fifth: why for the heavens did he say ״וַאֲדַבֵּרָה״ — harsh speech (dibbur), while for the earth he said ״אִמְרֵי פִי״ — soft speech (amirah)? Sixth: why did he specify ״אִמְרֵי פִי״ and not say ״אֲמָרַי״?" "<b>And I observed</b> to our Rabbis of blessed memory (in Sifrei) who said: Moses, who was close to the heavens, said ״הַאֲזִינוּ הַשָּׁמַיִם״ etc.; Isaiah, who was far from the heavens, said ״שִׁמְעוּ שָׁמַיִם״ (Isaiah 1:2). End quote. And according to their words, some of the fine points are resolved — not all of them." "And aside from their approach, it appears possible to say the following. But first we shall gain understanding into the meaning of the word ״הַאֲזִינוּ״. Now, it bears two paths: one, incline the ear; and the second, let your ear hearken. According to the first path, it indicates that the hearer is distant and needs to incline his ear in order to be able to hear, for without inclining his ear he cannot hear. And according to the second path, it indicates that the ear of the hearer is so close to the one causing it to be heard that for this he said הַאֲזֵן. And this second path is what our Rabbis of blessed memory chose. Now, since the word also bears the first path, and there is no decisive ruling, we shall follow it — and through this all the fine points will be properly resolved." "<b>And although</b> our predecessors chose another path, we have already said that there are seventy faces to the Torah (Numbers Rabbah 13:16), and in the realm of Aggadah one may interpret even if the interpretation is contrary to their words, as long as the contradiction is not in a matter of halakhah — and all the more so since we can maintain both paths in the verse, as I shall explain presently:" "<b>Behold,</b> the path by which we resolve all the fine points is this: since the heavens are above, according to the plain sense of the matter, therefore Moses called to them to incline their ear, and said to them ״הַאֲזִינוּ״ — meaning, incline your ears to hear my words. And to the earth he said ״וְתִשְׁמַע״ — meaning, of herself, without inclining the ear, for she stands before Him and is able to hear the very cutting of the utterance as it leaves His mouth — which is what he meant by ״אִמְרֵי פִי״ — since she is so close to Him." "<b>And by this approach</b> we have resolved all the fine points, for since one is far and one is near, therefore he apportioned the address to each one according to what is fitting for it. To the far one he called out that he should incline his ear, and he undertook upon himself to call in a loud voice — and that is his saying ״וַאֲדַבֵּרָה״. And to the near one, according to what is fitting for it, he alerted him that he too would speak to him, except that he does not need a call, for of himself he will hear even without calling in a loud voice, but rather through the cutting of the letters from his mouth it will be heard in him." "<b>And by this approach</b> the words of Isaiah will be reconciled, who said (Isaiah 1:2): ״שִׁמְעוּ שָׁמַיִם וְהַאֲזִינִי אָרֶץ״ — that he gave hearing to the heavens even though they are far from him, and hearkening to the earth even though it is near. The reason is, as the master speaker Isaiah himself concluded and said: ״כִּי ה׳ דִּבֵּר״ — meaning, the reason he said it to them in this order is that HaShem spoke, and in relation to the speaker, who is the Holy One blessed be He, the heavens are closer, as it is written (Psalms 115:16): ״הַשָּׁמַיִם שָׁמַיִם לַה׳״, and it is written (Isaiah 66:1): ״הַשָּׁמַיִם כִּסְאִי וְהָאָרֶץ הֲדוֹם רַגְלִי״. Therefore he said ״שִׁמְעוּ שָׁמַיִם״ — what HaShem spoke, for they do not need inclining of the ear; and ״הַאֲזִינִי אָרֶץ״ — because it is far, it needs to incline its ear to hear." "<b>Or perhaps</b> the word ״הַאֲזִינוּ״ may be justified in both ways, on this basis: in relation to the aspect of the mouth of Moses, which cuts the letters, the intention will be according to our words; and in relation to his soul, which is the spirituality, the intention will be according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory. And the matter itself proves this, for it was regarding the soul of Moses that our Sages of blessed memory said (Sifrei Ha'azinu 1) — because he was close to the heavens, for his essence — in the earth he would speak. And one cannot deny what is tangible." "<b>Further</b>, it may be intended that he spoke with the two components from which man is built: one, the spiritual part, which is classified under the name of the heavens; and one, the bodily part, which is classified under the name of the earth — in the manner that our Sages of blessed memory alluded (Sanhedrin 91a) in the verse: ״יִקְרָא אֶל הַשָּׁמַיִם מֵעָל״ etc. (Psalms 50:4)." "<b>And when he spoke</b> to the spirituality within man, he called to it and said: ״הַאֲזִינוּ הַשָּׁמַיִם״ — meaning, the heavenly parts, because they are hidden in concealment, therefore he called them to hearken. And he said against it a harsh speech, for the soul will endure bitterness in matters of the Creator, for it recognizes its obligation." "<b>And corresponding to</b> the body, which is in the revealed realm, he said ״וְתִשְׁמַע הָאָרֶץ״ — meaning, the parts of the earth — ״אִמְרֵי פִי״, for the body, since its nature is far removed from the intelligibles, needs a soft utterance in order to hear." "<b>Further,</b> he intends to speak to the gradations found within Israel: one, their great ones and leaders; second, the mass of the people who have no name in the nation. He designated the great ones by the name heavens, for they are on a lofty level, and he gave them precedence and addressed them with the command ״הַאֲזִינוּ הַשָּׁמַיִם״, and afterward said ״וְתִשְׁמַע הָאָרֶץ״ — meaning, of themselves the multitudes, who are likened to earth, will hear, when they see their great ones walking in the right path, as they said of blessed memory (Zohar vol. II 36a), and this is their language: אִי רֵישָׁא דְּעַמָּא אִתְתַּקַּן כּוֹלָּא אִתְתַּקַּן — if the head of the people is established, all is established." "<b>Further,</b> he alludes: if they give ear to words of Torah, then they will be called heavens — and this is his saying ״הַאֲזִינוּ״: by this you are accounted as heavens, along the lines of what is said (Psalms 82:6) ״אֲנִי אָמַרְתִּי אֱלֹהִים אַתֶּם״, and as we elaborated in parashat Bereshit, that through the deeds of the righteous even matter is transformed and they make it into form, and through the deeds of the wicked form is transformed into matter." "<b>And we have already said</b> that form is classified under the name heavens, and matter is classified under the name earth. And his saying ״וַאֲדַבֵּרָה״ — meaning: not only in a time of goodness shall you give ear, but even in a time when He speaks harshly in reproofs for sin, you must hearken." "And his saying <b>וְתִשְׁמַע הָאָרֶץ וְגוֹ׳</b> — meaning, that even after the soul's departure from the body and only the aspect of earth remains alone, even so the earth shall hear אִמְרֵי פִי. That is, along the lines of what they say in the Zohar (Zohar Ḥadash, Bereshit, on the verse ״ויאמר אלהים יהי מארת״), and this is its language: ״צְאֶינָה וּרְאֶינָה״ etc. — in the future a heavenly voice will resound in every single graveyard and say ״צְאֶינָה וּרְאֶינָה״, פּוּקוּ מִתְּחוֹת בְּלֵיאוּתָא דְּעַפְרָא וְאִתְּעָרוּ מִשְּׁנַתְכוֹן בְּנוֹת צִיּוֹן — emerge from beneath the weight of the dust and arouse from your sleep, daughters of Zion, as you say ״מָה הַצִּיּוּן הַלָּז״ (II Kings 23:17), end of quote. This is the utterance in which HaShem promises the righteous that their body will hear after it has become earth, and this is His saying ״וְתִשְׁמַע הָאָרֶץ אִמְרֵי פִי״. And from the inference of the matter you learn that whoever does not give ear to the word of HaShem — his body, which dwells in the earth, will not hear the voice of that crier, and his dust will not be stirred."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:21:41.899146239Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}